var e={id:`stoich`,objectives:[`Define and use relative atomic, isotopic, molecular and formula masses`,`Interpret mass spectra in terms of isotopic abundances and molecular fragments`,`Apply the mole concept and Avogadro constant`,`Determine empirical and molecular formulae from composition or combustion data`,`Construct balanced chemical equations including state symbols`,`Perform stoichiometric calculations involving masses, gas volumes, and solution concentrations`,`Carry out and analyse acid-base titrations including indicator choice`,`Calculate percentage yield and percentage purity`],chapters:[{id:`s1`,title:`Relative Atomic, Isotopic, Molecular Mass`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Atomic masses are tiny — 10⁻²³ grams. Working with such numbers directly would be miserable, so chemists adopt RELATIVE masses on a scale where ¹²C = 12 exactly. The carbon-12 standard is just a definition; what matters is that all other atomic masses are then expressed as ratios to this.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Relative atomic mass (Aᵣ)`,body:`The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element, on a scale where the mass of one ¹²C atom is exactly 12. Has no units.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Relative isotopic mass`,body:`The mass of one isotope of an element relative to ¹²C = 12. Each isotope has a single isotopic mass; the element's Aᵣ is the weighted mean across all natural isotopes.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Relative molecular mass (Mᵣ)`,body:`The weighted mean mass of a molecule on the ¹²C scale. Found by adding the Aᵣ values of all atoms in the molecule.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`Relative formula mass`,body:`Same idea as Mᵣ but used for ionic compounds (which have no discrete molecules). E.g. NaCl: 23 + 35.5 = 58.5.`},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: H₂SO₄`},{type:`p`,text:`Mᵣ = 2(1) + 32 + 4(16) = 2 + 32 + 64 = 98.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`All masses are RELATIVE — on the ¹²C = 12 scale.`,`Aᵣ is the weighted mean over isotopes — usually NOT an integer.`,`Mᵣ = sum of Aᵣ values for all atoms in the formula.`]}]},{id:`s2`,title:`Mass Spectra & Isotopic Abundances`,minutes:13,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`A mass spectrometer separates ionised particles by their mass-to-charge ratio (m/z). It tells us two important things: which isotopes an element has, and the exact masses of molecules and their fragments. The output is a graph of relative abundance vs m/z.`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:220,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`MASS SPECTRUM OF CHLORINE`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`line`,x1:40,y1:180,x2:300,y2:180,color:`dim`,width:1},{p:`line`,x1:40,y1:40,x2:40,y2:180,color:`dim`,width:1},{p:`text`,x:32,y:100,text:`% abundance`,color:`dim`,size:10,anchor:`end`},{p:`text`,x:170,y:200,text:`m/z`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`rect`,x:100,y:60,w:14,h:120,color:`primary`,stroke:.7,fill:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:107,y:195,text:`35`,color:`text`,size:9},{p:`text`,x:107,y:52,text:`75 %`,color:`text`,size:9},{p:`rect`,x:180,y:140,w:14,h:40,color:`primary`,stroke:.7,fill:`primary`},{p:`text`,x:187,y:195,text:`37`,color:`text`,size:9},{p:`text`,x:187,y:132,text:`25 %`,color:`text`,size:9},{p:`rect`,x:240,y:155,w:8,h:25,color:`dim`,stroke:.7,fill:`dim`},{p:`text`,x:244,y:195,text:`70`,color:`dim`,size:8},{p:`rect`,x:255,y:160,w:8,h:20,color:`dim`,stroke:.7,fill:`dim`},{p:`text`,x:259,y:195,text:`72`,color:`dim`,size:8},{p:`rect`,x:270,y:172,w:8,h:8,color:`dim`,stroke:.7,fill:`dim`},{p:`text`,x:274,y:195,text:`74`,color:`dim`,size:8},{p:`text`,x:258,y:148,text:`Cl₂⁺ peaks`,color:`dim`,size:8}]}]},caption:`Mass spectrum of chlorine (atoms and Cl₂ molecules)`},{type:`h`,text:`How a mass spectrometer works (overview)`},{type:`ul`,items:[`IONISATION — the sample is bombarded with electrons (electron impact) or sprayed and protonated (electrospray). Result: positive ions.`,`ACCELERATION — a voltage accelerates the ions to a known kinetic energy.`,`DEFLECTION — a magnetic field bends the path; lighter ions deflect more.`,`DETECTION — a detector counts ions arriving at each m/z. Output: peaks at integer m/z values with relative heights = relative abundances.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reading the spectrum of an ELEMENT`},{type:`p`,text:`Each peak corresponds to one isotope of the element. Peak position = mass of the isotope (m/z, since z = +1 in most cases). Peak height = relative abundance. Use the data to calculate Aᵣ as a weighted mean.`},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: chlorine`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Two peaks: m/z = 35 (75 %) and m/z = 37 (25 %).`,`Aᵣ(Cl) = (35 × 75 + 37 × 25) / 100 = (2625 + 925) / 100 = 35.5.`,`Confirms the periodic-table value of 35.5.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reading the spectrum of a MOLECULE`},{type:`p`,text:`In addition to the isotope peaks at low m/z, a molecule also shows the molecular ion peak at m/z = Mᵣ — this is the parent molecule with one electron knocked off, M⁺. Fragments appear at lower m/z values (smaller pieces of the molecule). For Cl₂ you see peaks at m/z = 70 (³⁵Cl-³⁵Cl), 72 (³⁵Cl-³⁷Cl), 74 (³⁷Cl-³⁷Cl) in ratio 9:6:1 reflecting the 3:1 abundance ratio of the two Cl isotopes.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`M+1 peak from ¹³C`,body:`Carbon is mostly ¹²C (98.9 %) but contains a small amount of ¹³C (1.1 %). For a molecule with n carbons, the M+1 peak is approximately 1.1 × n % of the M peak. Useful for counting carbons in unknown organics.`},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`M+2 peak signals Cl or Br`,body:`A molecule containing one chlorine atom shows an M+2 peak about ⅓ the height of the M peak (³⁵Cl:³⁷Cl ≈ 3:1). One bromine: M+2 about equal to M (⁷⁹Br:⁸¹Br ≈ 1:1). This is a fast diagnostic.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`m/z = mass for singly-charged ions.`,`Peak height = relative abundance.`,`Aᵣ = Σ(mass × abundance) / 100.`,`M+1 hints at carbon count; M+2 signals Cl or Br.`]}]},{id:`s3`,title:`The Mole and Avogadro Constant`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Atoms and molecules are too small to handle individually. Chemists use a counting unit, the mole, which packages exactly 6.022 × 10²³ particles together. The mole bridges the world of single atoms (which we cannot weigh) and the world of laboratory grams (which we can).`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:200,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:18,text:`THE MOLE — A COUNTING UNIT`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`circle`,cx:160,cy:100,r:48,color:`primary`,stroke:1.5,fill:`primary`,fillAlpha:.13},{p:`text`,x:160,y:92,text:`1 mole`,color:`text`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:110,text:`= 6.022×10²³`,color:`primary`,size:13,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:128,text:`particles`,color:`dim`,size:9},{p:`text`,x:160,y:170,text:`Avogadro constant Nₐ`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:160,y:186,text:`= number of atoms in exactly 12 g of ¹²C`,color:`dim`,size:9}]}]},caption:`Definition of the mole`},{type:`callout`,kind:`definition`,title:`The mole`,body:`A mole of any substance contains exactly Avogadro's number Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ elementary entities (atoms, molecules, ions, electrons — whichever is specified).`},{type:`h`,text:`Why 6.022 × 10²³?`},{type:`p`,text:`It is the number of carbon-12 atoms in exactly 12 grams of pure ¹²C. The unit was chosen so that the mass of one mole of an element in grams (the molar mass M) equals its relative atomic mass Aᵣ (a dimensionless number). This is a deliberate convenience.`},{type:`h`,text:`Three key conversions`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:200,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`THE MOLE TRIANGLE`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`path`,d:`M160 40 L80 160 L240 160 Z`,color:`primary`,width:2},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:120,x2:160,y2:160,color:`primary`,width:1},{p:`text`,x:160,y:100,text:`m`,color:`text`,size:16,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:120,y:155,text:`n`,color:`text`,size:14,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:200,y:155,text:`M`,color:`text`,size:14,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:185,text:`n = m / M`,color:`accent`,size:11,bold:!0}]}]},caption:`n = m / M`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Mass ↔ moles:    n = m / M     (m in grams, M in g/mol)`,`Number ↔ moles:  N = n × Nₐ`,`Volume of gas ↔ moles (at RTP):  n = V / 24    (V in dm³)`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked example`},{type:`ul`,items:[`How many moles in 18 g of water?  n = 18 / 18 = 1 mol.`,`How many H₂O molecules?  N = 1 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 6.022 × 10²³.`,`How many H atoms?  Each H₂O has 2 H, so 2 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 1.2 × 10²⁴.`]},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`1 mol = Nₐ = 6.022 × 10²³ particles.`,`Molar mass M (g/mol) numerically equals Aᵣ or Mᵣ.`,`Master n = m/M — the gateway to all stoichiometry.`]}]},{id:`s4`,title:`Empirical and Molecular Formulae`,minutes:13,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The empirical formula is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound. The molecular formula is the actual number of atoms in one molecule. Sometimes they are the same (CO₂); often they differ by a whole-number multiplier (empirical CH; molecular C₆H₆ = (CH)₆ for benzene).`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:220,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`FINDING THE FORMULA`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:34,w:280,h:28,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:53,text:`1. Mass or % of each element`,color:`text`,size:11},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:62,x2:160,y2:72,color:`primary`,width:1},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:74,w:280,h:28,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:93,text:`2. Divide by Aᵣ → moles of each`,color:`text`,size:11},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:102,x2:160,y2:112,color:`primary`,width:1},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:114,w:280,h:28,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:133,text:`3. Divide by smallest → ratio`,color:`text`,size:11},{p:`line`,x1:160,y1:142,x2:160,y2:152,color:`primary`,width:1},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:154,w:280,h:28,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:173,text:`4. Round to whole numbers → empirical`,color:`text`,size:11},{p:`text`,x:160,y:205,text:`If Mᵣ given: molecular formula = empirical × (Mᵣ ÷ empirical mass)`,color:`accent`,size:10,bold:!0}]}]},caption:`The four-step procedure`},{type:`h`,text:`From composition by mass — worked`},{type:`p`,text:`A compound contains 40.0 % C, 6.7 % H, 53.3 % O. Find its empirical formula.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`STEP 1 — assume 100 g sample. Then 40.0 g C, 6.7 g H, 53.3 g O.`,`STEP 2 — divide by Aᵣ. nC = 40.0/12 = 3.33, nH = 6.7/1 = 6.7, nO = 53.3/16 = 3.33.`,`STEP 3 — divide by smallest (3.33). C : 1, H : 2.01, O : 1.`,`STEP 4 — round → CH₂O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`From combustion data — worked`},{type:`p`,text:`Burning 1.50 g of an organic compound containing only C, H, and O gives 2.20 g CO₂ and 0.90 g H₂O. Find the empirical formula.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Mass of C = 2.20 × (12/44) = 0.60 g (all C in CO₂ came from the compound).`,`Mass of H = 0.90 × (2/18) = 0.10 g (all H in H₂O came from the compound).`,`Mass of O in compound = 1.50 − 0.60 − 0.10 = 0.80 g.`,`Moles: C = 0.60/12 = 0.05, H = 0.10/1 = 0.10, O = 0.80/16 = 0.05.`,`Ratio: 1 : 2 : 1  →  empirical formula CH₂O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`From empirical to molecular`},{type:`p`,text:`If you know the relative molecular mass Mᵣ:  ratio n = Mᵣ / (empirical-formula mass). Multiply each subscript in the empirical formula by n. Example: empirical CH₂O has mass 30. If Mᵣ = 180, then n = 180/30 = 6, so molecular formula is C₆H₁₂O₆ (glucose).`},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Common slip — non-integer ratios`,body:`If after dividing by the smallest mole value you get something like 1 : 1.5, multiply ALL ratios by 2 to clear the fraction. 1 : 1.5 → 2 : 3. Don't round 1.5 to 2.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`Procedure: composition → moles → ratio → empirical.`,`Combustion: all C went to CO₂, all H went to H₂O, find O by difference.`,`Molecular = empirical × (Mᵣ ÷ empirical mass).`]}]},{id:`s5`,title:`Balanced Equations & Reaction Ratios`,minutes:11,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`A balanced chemical equation is the stoichiometric blueprint of a reaction. The coefficients tell you the mole ratio in which species react. Mass is always conserved — same total atoms either side. Charge is also conserved in ionic equations.`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:180,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`BALANCED EQUATIONS — MASS IS CONSERVED`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:50,text:`2 H₂  +  O₂  →  2 H₂O`,color:`text`,size:14,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:80,y:85,text:`LEFT`,color:`text`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:80,y:105,text:`4 H, 2 O`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:240,y:85,text:`RIGHT`,color:`text`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:240,y:105,text:`4 H, 2 O`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:160,y:140,text:`moles ratio: 2 : 1 : 2`,color:`accent`,size:10,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:160,text:`coefficients tell you reaction stoichiometry`,color:`dim`,size:9}]}]},caption:`Mass and atoms balance both sides`},{type:`h`,text:`Balancing — a simple workflow`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Write reactants and products with correct formulae (don't alter formulae!).`,`Balance atoms one element at a time. Save H and O for last when an organic compound burns.`,`Use fractions if needed, then multiply to clear them.`,`Add state symbols (s), (l), (g), (aq) where required.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: combustion of propane`},{type:`ul`,items:[`C₃H₈ + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O.`,`Carbons: 3 CO₂. Hydrogens: 4 H₂O.`,`Oxygens on right: 3(2) + 4(1) = 10. Need 5 O₂.`,`Final: C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 3 CO₂ + 4 H₂O.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Reaction ratios — using the coefficients`},{type:`p`,text:`Once balanced, the coefficients ARE the mole ratio. For C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 3 CO₂ + 4 H₂O: 1 mole of C₃H₈ requires 5 moles of O₂ and produces 3 moles of CO₂ and 4 moles of H₂O. Use this ratio to convert between any two reactants or products.`},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: limiting reagent`},{type:`p`,text:`Mix 4 mol H₂ with 1 mol O₂ in 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Which runs out first?`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Stoichiometry needs 2 mol H₂ per 1 mol O₂.`,`4 mol H₂ requires 2 mol O₂. We only have 1 mol O₂. So O₂ is the limiting reagent.`,`O₂ runs out first; only 2 mol H₂ react; 2 mol H₂ left over; 2 mol H₂O produced.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Use the LIMITING reagent in calculations`,body:`The limiting reagent dictates how much product can form. Excess reagent quantities are irrelevant for product calculation. Always identify limiting reagent first when amounts of both reactants are given.`}]},{id:`s6`,title:`Calculations — Masses & Gas Volumes`,minutes:14,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`The link between balanced equations and laboratory measurements is the mole. The general workflow is: mass-or-volume → moles → use ratio → moles of unknown → mass-or-volume of unknown.`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:180,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`MOLAR GAS VOLUME`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:50,text:`1 mol gas at RTP = 24.0 dm³`,color:`text`,size:13,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:70,text:`(RTP = 25 °C, 100 kPa)`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:160,y:100,text:`1 mol gas at STP = 22.4 dm³`,color:`text`,size:13,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:120,text:`(STP = 0 °C, 100 kPa)`,color:`dim`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:160,y:150,text:`n = V / 24    (at RTP, V in dm³)`,color:`accent`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:170,text:`Or use PV = nRT for non-standard conditions`,color:`dim`,size:9}]}]},caption:`Molar gas volume at standard conditions`},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: reacting masses`},{type:`p`,text:`What mass of CO₂ is produced when 11 g of propane is burned in excess oxygen?`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 3 CO₂ + 4 H₂O.`,`Moles of propane: n = 11 / 44 = 0.25 mol.`,`Ratio C₃H₈ : CO₂ = 1 : 3, so moles of CO₂ = 0.25 × 3 = 0.75 mol.`,`Mass of CO₂: m = n × M = 0.75 × 44 = 33 g.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: gas volumes at RTP`},{type:`p`,text:`What volume of CO₂ at RTP is produced from the propane above?`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Moles of CO₂ = 0.75 mol (from above).`,`Volume = n × 24 = 0.75 × 24 = 18 dm³.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Working with PV = nRT for non-standard conditions`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Pressure P in Pa (or use kPa with R = 8.314 if V is in dm³).`,`Volume V in m³ (or dm³).`,`Temperature T in KELVIN — always convert (K = °C + 273).`,`R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: PV = nRT`},{type:`p`,text:`Calculate the volume of 0.50 mol O₂ at 27 °C and 100 kPa.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`T = 27 + 273 = 300 K. P = 100 000 Pa. n = 0.50.`,`V = nRT/P = 0.50 × 8.314 × 300 / 100 000 = 1247.1 / 100 000 = 0.01247 m³ = 12.47 dm³.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Unit hygiene`,body:`The single biggest source of marks lost. ALWAYS write units alongside numbers in your working. Convert °C to K. Convert dm³ to m³ (or use kPa with R = 8.314 carefully). Quote answers to a sensible number of significant figures (usually 3 sf).`}]},{id:`s7`,title:`Concentrations & Titrations`,minutes:13,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`For solutions, concentration links volume to moles. Concentration c = n/V (V in dm³ for mol/dm³). Titrations use this relationship to determine an unknown concentration by reaction with a standard solution of known concentration.`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:240,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`ACID-BASE TITRATION`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`rect`,x:155,y:35,w:14,h:100,color:`text`,stroke:1},{p:`line`,x1:162,y1:135,x2:162,y2:148,color:`text`,width:1.5},{p:`text`,x:120,y:50,text:`burette`,color:`text`,size:10,anchor:`end`},{p:`text`,x:120,y:64,text:`acid (known c)`,color:`dim`,size:9,anchor:`end`},{p:`path`,d:`M135 160 L145 200 L175 200 L185 160 Z`,color:`text`,width:1.2},{p:`line`,x1:135,y1:160,x2:185,y2:160,color:`text`,width:1},{p:`path`,d:`M142 190 C142 180 178 180 178 190 C178 200 142 200 142 190 Z`,color:`primary`,width:.5,fill:`#4361ee40`},{p:`text`,x:210,y:185,text:`conical flask`,color:`text`,size:10,anchor:`start`},{p:`text`,x:210,y:199,text:`base + indicator`,color:`dim`,size:9,anchor:`start`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:225,text:`endpoint: indicator changes colour`,color:`accent`,size:10,bold:!0}]}]},caption:`Titration setup`},{type:`h`,text:`The titration calculation`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Identify the balanced equation and the mole ratio between titrant and analyte.`,`Use moles_titrant = c_titrant × V_titrant to find moles of titrant added.`,`Apply the mole ratio to find moles of analyte.`,`Divide by the analyte volume to get c_analyte.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: NaOH neutralised by HCl`},{type:`p`,text:`A 25.0 cm³ sample of NaOH(aq) of unknown concentration is titrated with 0.10 mol/dm³ HCl(aq). The endpoint is at 22.5 cm³ of HCl. Find [NaOH].`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O.   Ratio 1 : 1.`,`Moles HCl = c × V = 0.10 × (22.5/1000) = 0.10 × 0.0225 = 2.25 × 10⁻³ mol.`,`Moles NaOH = 2.25 × 10⁻³ mol (1:1 ratio).`,`[NaOH] = n / V = 2.25 × 10⁻³ / (25.0/1000) = 2.25 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.090 mol/dm³.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Choosing an indicator`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Strong acid + strong base — wide pH jump — methyl orange OR phenolphthalein both work.`,`Strong acid + weak base — pH range mostly acidic — methyl orange.`,`Weak acid + strong base — pH range mostly alkaline — phenolphthalein.`,`Weak acid + weak base — no sharp jump — DON'T use indicators; use a pH meter.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Mind your units`,body:`Concentration in mol/dm³ goes with volume in DM³, not cm³. Convert cm³ to dm³ by dividing by 1000. The most common arithmetic mistake on this topic is forgetting that division.`},{type:`h`,text:`Standard solutions`},{type:`p`,text:`A primary standard is a substance that can be weighed accurately and dissolved to a known volume to give a precisely-known concentration. Examples: anhydrous Na₂CO₃, KIO₃, oxalic acid hydrate. Used to standardise reagents (like NaOH) which absorb moisture and CO₂ from air and so cannot be weighed precisely.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`c = n / V; remember V is in dm³ for mol/dm³.`,`Titration calc: moles titrant → ratio → moles analyte → concentration.`,`Indicator choice depends on the strengths of the acid and the base.`]}]},{id:`s8`,title:`Percentage Yield & Percentage Purity`,minutes:9,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Real reactions never produce 100 % of the predicted product, and starting materials are rarely 100 % pure. These two practical facts are quantified by percentage yield and percentage purity.`},{type:`d`,spec:{blocks:[{type:`raw`,height:200,primitives:[{p:`text`,x:160,y:16,text:`YIELD & PURITY`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:40,w:280,h:50,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:62,text:`% YIELD`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:80,text:`= (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`rect`,x:20,y:105,w:280,h:50,color:`primary`,stroke:1,fill:`panel`},{p:`text`,x:160,y:127,text:`% PURITY`,color:`primary`,size:11,bold:!0},{p:`text`,x:160,y:145,text:`= (mass of pure substance ÷ total mass) × 100`,color:`text`,size:10},{p:`text`,x:160,y:180,text:`both never exceed 100 % (in honest data!)`,color:`dim`,size:9}]}]},caption:`The two key formulas`},{type:`h`,text:`Percentage yield`},{type:`ul`,items:[`theoretical yield = mass of product expected from the balanced equation (assuming complete conversion).`,`actual yield = mass of product actually obtained from the experiment.`,`% yield = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Why yield is rarely 100 %`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Reaction may be reversible — equilibrium prevents complete conversion.`,`Side reactions consume some reactant but produce different products.`,`Loss during transfer, filtration, recrystallisation.`,`Some product remains dissolved or sticks to apparatus.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: yield`},{type:`p`,text:`4.0 g of CaCO₃ is heated until decomposition is complete. The CaO obtained is 1.96 g. Calculate the percentage yield.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Equation: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.`,`Moles CaCO₃ = 4.0 / 100 = 0.040 mol → theoretical moles CaO = 0.040.`,`Theoretical mass CaO = 0.040 × 56 = 2.24 g.`,`% yield = (1.96 / 2.24) × 100 = 87.5 %.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Percentage purity`},{type:`ul`,items:[`mass of pure substance / total mass of impure sample × 100.`,`Often determined by reacting a measured mass of impure sample with a standard reagent and back-calculating how much of the desired substance must have been present.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Worked: purity`},{type:`p`,text:`5.00 g of impure NaOH is dissolved in water and titrated against 1.00 mol/dm³ HCl. The endpoint is at 100 cm³. Find the percentage purity.`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Moles HCl = c × V = 1.00 × (100/1000) = 0.100 mol.`,`Mole ratio NaOH : HCl = 1 : 1, so moles NaOH = 0.100 mol.`,`Mass of pure NaOH = 0.100 × 40 = 4.00 g.`,`% purity = (4.00 / 5.00) × 100 = 80.0 %.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`warning`,title:`Plausibility check`,body:`Always sanity-check your answer. % purity should sit between 0 and 100. If it comes out as 1 % or 200 %, you have a units error somewhere — usually the cm³ → dm³ conversion.`},{type:`takeaway`,items:[`% yield = (actual ÷ theoretical) × 100. Honest values < 100 %.`,`% purity = (pure ÷ total impure) × 100. Use a quantitative reaction to back-calculate.`]}]},{id:`s9`,title:`Calculation Strategy & Exam Tips`,minutes:8,blocks:[{type:`p`,text:`Stoichiometry questions punish sloppy working and reward systematic technique. Internalise the workflow once, then it scales to every problem.`},{type:`h`,text:`The universal recipe`},{type:`ul`,items:[`1. WRITE the balanced equation. (Or balance it from scratch.)`,`2. EXTRACT the data: which masses, volumes, concentrations are given?`,`3. CONVERT every given quantity to MOLES using the right conversion (n=m/M, n=cV, n=V/24).`,`4. APPLY the mole ratio from the equation to find moles of the unknown.`,`5. CONVERT the unknown's moles back to mass, volume or concentration.`,`6. CHECK units, significant figures, and plausibility.`]},{type:`h`,text:`Common pitfalls`},{type:`ul`,items:[`Forgetting to divide cm³ by 1000 to convert to dm³.`,`Forgetting to convert °C to K when using PV = nRT.`,`Assuming a 1:1 mole ratio when the equation says otherwise.`,`Quoting answers to too many or too few significant figures.`,`Not identifying the limiting reagent when both reactant amounts are given.`,`Adding moles instead of using the ratio.`]},{type:`callout`,kind:`key`,title:`Significant figures`,body:`Quote your final answer to the same number of sig figs as the LEAST precise piece of given data, usually 3 sig figs at A-level. Carry extra digits through working to avoid rounding error, only round at the end.`}]}],quizzes:[{q:`Relative atomic mass is defined relative to:`,opts:[`¹H = 1`,`¹⁶O = 16`,`¹²C = 12 exactly`,`Average of all elements`],ans:2,hint:`Carbon-12 scale.`},{q:`The relative molecular mass of H₂SO₄ is:`,opts:[`82`,`98`,`49`,`96`],ans:1,hint:`2(1) + 32 + 4(16).`},{q:`Avogadro's number is:`,opts:[`1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹`,`6.022 × 10²³`,`8.314`,`273`],ans:1,hint:`Particles per mole.`},{q:`Number of moles of O atoms in 88 g of CO₂:`,opts:[`1`,`2`,`4`,`88`],ans:2,hint:`n(CO₂) = 88/44 = 2 mol; each has 2 O atoms.`},{q:`A mass spectrum of an element shows peaks at m/z 24 (79 %), 25 (10 %), 26 (11 %). Aᵣ is:`,opts:[`24.0`,`24.32`,`25.0`,`25.32`],ans:1,hint:`Σ(mass × abundance)/100.`},{q:`A molecule shows an M+2 peak about ⅓ the height of M. The molecule probably contains:`,opts:[`Carbon`,`One Cl atom`,`One Br atom`,`Sulfur`],ans:1,hint:`³⁵Cl:³⁷Cl ≈ 3:1.`},{q:`A molecule with one bromine atom shows an M+2 peak with intensity:`,opts:[`Tiny`,`About ⅓ of M`,`Approximately equal to M`,`Three times M`],ans:2,hint:`⁷⁹Br:⁸¹Br ≈ 1:1.`},{q:`A compound is 40 % C, 6.7 % H, 53.3 % O. Empirical formula:`,opts:[`CH₂O`,`C₂H₄O₂`,`CHO`,`C₂HO`],ans:0,hint:`Divide by Aᵣ, then by smallest.`},{q:`A compound has empirical CH₂O and Mᵣ = 180. Molecular formula:`,opts:[`CH₂O`,`C₃H₆O₃`,`C₆H₁₂O₆`,`C₂H₄O₂`],ans:2,hint:`Multiplier = 180/30 = 6.`},{q:`In combustion analysis: 1.50 g compound → 2.20 g CO₂. Mass of C in compound:`,opts:[`0.30 g`,`0.60 g`,`1.20 g`,`2.20 g`],ans:1,hint:`Mass C = mass CO₂ × (12/44).`},{q:`Balanced equation: 2 H₂ + O₂ → 2 H₂O. From 4 mol H₂ + 1 mol O₂, the limiting reagent is:`,opts:[`H₂`,`O₂`,`Both at the same time`,`Water`],ans:1,hint:`Need 2 mol H₂ per O₂; 4 mol H₂ would need 2 mol O₂.`},{q:`Burn 0.5 mol C₃H₈. Moles of CO₂ produced:`,opts:[`0.5`,`1.5`,`3`,`5`],ans:1,hint:`C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 3 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Ratio 1:3.`},{q:`Volume of 1 mol gas at RTP is approximately:`,opts:[`2.4 dm³`,`24 dm³`,`240 dm³`,`24 cm³`],ans:1,hint:`24 dm³ at 25°C, 100 kPa.`},{q:`Volume of 0.25 mol N₂ at RTP:`,opts:[`6 dm³`,`24 dm³`,`0.96 dm³`,`12 dm³`],ans:0,hint:`V = n × 24 = 0.25 × 24.`},{q:`PV = nRT: temperature must be in:`,opts:[`°C`,`K`,`F`,`Either`],ans:1,hint:`Always Kelvin.`},{q:`A gas at 100 kPa and 27 °C contains 0.5 mol. Volume (R = 8.314):`,opts:[`12.5 dm³`,`125 dm³`,`1.25 dm³`,`0.125 dm³`],ans:0,hint:`V = nRT/P; T = 300 K, V in m³ then × 1000 for dm³.`},{q:`Concentration in mol/dm³ requires volume in:`,opts:[`cm³`,`dm³`,`m³`,`mL`],ans:1,hint:`Match units.`},{q:`How many moles of NaOH in 25.0 cm³ of 0.10 mol/dm³ NaOH?`,opts:[`2.5 × 10⁻³`,`0.025`,`0.10`,`25`],ans:0,hint:`n = c × V = 0.10 × 0.025.`},{q:`Titrating 25 cm³ of 0.20 mol/dm³ NaOH against HCl needs 20 cm³ HCl. [HCl] is:`,opts:[`0.16`,`0.20`,`0.25`,`0.40`],ans:2,hint:`n NaOH = 0.20 × 0.025 = 0.005 mol = n HCl. c = 0.005/0.020.`},{q:`For a strong acid + weak base titration, best indicator is:`,opts:[`Phenolphthalein`,`Methyl orange`,`Litmus`,`Universal`],ans:1,hint:`pH range mostly acidic — methyl orange (3.1-4.4).`},{q:`Why use a primary standard?`,opts:[`Cheaper`,`Pure, stable, can be weighed accurately`,`Strongly coloured`,`Reacts only once`],ans:1,hint:`Need precise concentration to standardise others.`},{q:`What does percentage yield measure?`,opts:[`Purity of product`,`(Actual ÷ theoretical) × 100`,`How fast reaction is`,`Amount of catalyst`],ans:1,hint:`Compares what you got to what you expected.`},{q:`Why is yield often less than 100 %?`,opts:[`Reaction is too fast`,`Side reactions, losses on transfer, equilibrium`,`Catalysts decompose`,`Atoms vanish`],ans:1,hint:`Mass is conserved, but products are lost in real labs.`},{q:`Decomposing 4.0 g CaCO₃ gives 1.96 g CaO. % yield is:`,opts:[`49 %`,`87.5 %`,`100 %`,`110 %`],ans:1,hint:`theoretical CaO = 0.04 × 56 = 2.24 g.`},{q:`5.0 g of impure NaOH is titrated with 1.0 mol/dm³ HCl needing 100 cm³ to reach endpoint. % purity is:`,opts:[`40 %`,`80 %`,`100 %`,`20 %`],ans:1,hint:`n HCl = 0.10 mol = n NaOH. Mass NaOH = 4.0 g. (4.0/5.0)×100.`},{q:`A reaction has actual yield 25 g and theoretical yield 50 g. % yield is:`,opts:[`25 %`,`50 %`,`75 %`,`100 %`],ans:1,hint:`25/50 × 100.`},{q:`In stoichiometric calculations, you ALWAYS first:`,opts:[`Convert to grams`,`Write a balanced equation`,`Calculate concentration`,`Identify the colour`],ans:1,hint:`No equation = no ratio = no calculation.`},{q:`A 5.0 g sample of impure CaCO₃ reacts with excess HCl to give 1.0 dm³ of CO₂ at RTP. % purity:`,opts:[`50 %`,`83 %`,`100 %`,`60 %`],ans:1,hint:`n CO₂ = 1.0/24 = 0.0417 mol → mass CaCO₃ = 0.0417 × 100 = 4.17 g. 4.17/5 ×100.`},{q:`For an acid that loses 2 H⁺ per molecule (e.g. H₂SO₄) titrated against NaOH, the mole ratio is:`,opts:[`1:1`,`1:2 (acid:base)`,`2:1`,`1:3`],ans:1,hint:`1 mol H₂SO₄ + 2 mol NaOH → 1 mol Na₂SO₄ + 2 mol H₂O.`},{q:`Which is true of a balanced equation?`,opts:[`Mass and atoms balance both sides`,`Volumes always balance`,`Mass is gained on the right`,`Coefficients are arbitrary`],ans:0,hint:`Conservation of mass.`}],multi:[],structured:[{title:`Mass spectrometry`,marks:8,q:`A mass spectrum of bromine shows peaks at m/z = 79 (50.7 %), 81 (49.3 %). For Br₂ molecules, peaks appear at m/z = 158, 160, 162.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Calculate the relative atomic mass of bromine.`,marks:2,ans:`Aᵣ = (79 × 50.7 + 81 × 49.3) / 100 = (4005.3 + 3993.3) / 100 = 79.99 ≈ 80.`},{q:`(b) Explain why Br₂ shows three peaks (158, 160, 162) and predict their relative ratio.`,marks:4,ans:`Br₂ contains two Br atoms, each independently ⁷⁹Br or ⁸¹Br with ~1:1 abundance. Possible combinations: ⁷⁹Br-⁷⁹Br (mass 158), ⁷⁹Br-⁸¹Br and ⁸¹Br-⁷⁹Br (both mass 160), ⁸¹Br-⁸¹Br (mass 162). Ratio = 1 : 2 : 1 (because the 79-81 combination has TWO ways of forming).`},{q:`(c) State briefly the role of each step in a mass spectrometer: ionisation, acceleration, deflection, detection.`,marks:2,ans:`Ionisation: produces positive ions (electron impact or electrospray). Acceleration: voltage gives ions known kinetic energy. Deflection: magnetic field bends ion paths; lighter ions deflect more. Detection: electrical detector counts ions arriving at each m/z.`}]},{title:`Empirical and molecular formula from combustion`,marks:8,q:`0.300 g of an organic compound contains C, H and O only. On complete combustion, 0.440 g of CO₂ and 0.180 g of H₂O are produced. The compound has Mᵣ = 60.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Calculate the masses of C and of H in the original sample.`,marks:2,ans:`Mass C = 0.440 × (12/44) = 0.120 g.  Mass H = 0.180 × (2/18) = 0.020 g.`},{q:`(b) Hence calculate the mass and the moles of O in the sample.`,marks:2,ans:`Mass O = 0.300 − 0.120 − 0.020 = 0.160 g.  Moles O = 0.160 / 16 = 0.0100 mol.`},{q:`(c) Determine the empirical formula.`,marks:2,ans:`Moles C = 0.120 / 12 = 0.0100. Moles H = 0.020 / 1 = 0.0200. Moles O = 0.0100. Divide by smallest: C : H : O = 1 : 2 : 1. Empirical formula: CH₂O.`},{q:`(d) Determine the molecular formula given Mᵣ = 60.`,marks:2,ans:`Empirical-formula mass (CH₂O) = 12 + 2 + 16 = 30. Multiplier = 60 / 30 = 2. Molecular formula = C₂H₄O₂. (This is ethanoic acid, CH₃COOH.)`}]},{title:`Stoichiometric calculation with gas`,marks:9,q:`When marble chips (CaCO₃) react with hydrochloric acid, carbon dioxide is evolved.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the balanced equation including state symbols.`,marks:2,ans:`CaCO₃(s) + 2 HCl(aq) → CaCl₂(aq) + H₂O(l) + CO₂(g)`},{q:`(b) Calculate the volume of CO₂ produced at RTP from 5.0 g of pure CaCO₃ in excess acid.`,marks:3,ans:`Moles CaCO₃ = 5.0 / 100 = 0.050 mol. Mole ratio CaCO₃ : CO₂ = 1 : 1, so moles CO₂ = 0.050. Volume = 0.050 × 24 = 1.2 dm³.`},{q:`(c) An impure sample of marble (mass 5.0 g) gives 1.0 dm³ of CO₂ at RTP under the same conditions. Calculate the percentage purity.`,marks:4,ans:`Moles CO₂ = 1.0 / 24 = 0.0417 mol. Moles CaCO₃ = 0.0417 mol. Mass of pure CaCO₃ = 0.0417 × 100 = 4.17 g. % purity = (4.17 / 5.0) × 100 = 83.3 %.`}]},{title:`Acid-base titration`,marks:8,q:`A 25.0 cm³ sample of vinegar (assumed to be aqueous ethanoic acid CH₃COOH) is diluted to 250 cm³ in a volumetric flask. A 25.0 cm³ portion of this dilute solution is titrated against 0.100 mol/dm³ NaOH; the endpoint is reached at 20.0 cm³.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Write the equation for the reaction between ethanoic acid and NaOH.`,marks:1,ans:`CH₃COOH(aq) + NaOH(aq) → CH₃COONa(aq) + H₂O(l)`},{q:`(b) Calculate the concentration of ethanoic acid in the DILUTE solution.`,marks:3,ans:`Moles NaOH = 0.100 × (20.0/1000) = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Mole ratio 1:1, so moles CH₃COOH (in the 25.0 cm³ titrated) = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. [CH₃COOH] = 2.00 × 10⁻³ / 0.0250 = 0.0800 mol/dm³.`},{q:`(c) Hence calculate the concentration of ethanoic acid in the ORIGINAL undiluted vinegar.`,marks:2,ans:`Vinegar was diluted by a factor of 250/25 = 10. So [CH₃COOH] in original vinegar = 0.0800 × 10 = 0.800 mol/dm³.`},{q:`(d) Suggest an indicator suitable for this titration and justify your choice.`,marks:2,ans:`Phenolphthalein is suitable (colour change pH 8.3-10). The titration is between a weak acid (CH₃COOH, Ka ~ 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) and a strong base (NaOH); the pH at the endpoint is slightly alkaline (because the salt CH₃COO⁻ is basic). Methyl orange would change colour too early (before the equivalence point) and so would not give a sharp endpoint.`}]},{title:`Yield in a multi-step reaction`,marks:6,q:`In a synthesis of aspirin (C₉H₈O₄, Mᵣ = 180) from salicylic acid (C₇H₆O₃, Mᵣ = 138), the equation is: C₇H₆O₃ + (CH₃CO)₂O → C₉H₈O₄ + CH₃COOH.`,parts:[{q:`(a) Calculate the maximum theoretical mass of aspirin that can be made from 5.00 g of salicylic acid in excess acetic anhydride.`,marks:2,ans:`Moles salicylic acid = 5.00 / 138 = 0.0362 mol. Mole ratio 1:1, so moles aspirin = 0.0362. Mass of aspirin = 0.0362 × 180 = 6.52 g.`},{q:`(b) The actual mass of aspirin obtained was 4.50 g. Calculate the percentage yield.`,marks:2,ans:`% yield = (4.50 / 6.52) × 100 = 69.0 %.`},{q:`(c) Suggest TWO reasons why the yield was less than 100 %.`,marks:2,ans:`Possible reasons: incomplete reaction (equilibrium); side reactions giving by-products; loss of product on filtration or recrystallisation; some product remained dissolved in solvent; transfer losses between glassware.`}]}],worked:[{title:`Calculate Aᵣ from a mass spectrum`,diff:`easy`,q:`A mass spectrum of magnesium shows peaks at m/z 24 (78.6 %), 25 (10.1 %), 26 (11.3 %). Calculate the relative atomic mass of magnesium.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Use Aᵣ = Σ(mass × abundance) / 100.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Aᵣ = (24 × 78.6 + 25 × 10.1 + 26 × 11.3) / 100 = (1886.4 + 252.5 + 293.8) / 100.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Aᵣ = 2432.7 / 100 = 24.327.`}],ans:`Aᵣ(Mg) = 24.33 (to 4 sf), matching the periodic-table value of 24.31.`},{title:`Combustion → empirical → molecular`,diff:`medium`,q:`Burning 0.46 g of an organic compound containing only C, H and O gives 0.88 g of CO₂ and 0.54 g of H₂O. The compound has Mᵣ = 46. Find the molecular formula.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Mass of C in original compound = 0.88 × (12/44) = 0.24 g. Mass of H = 0.54 × (2/18) = 0.06 g. Mass of O (by difference) = 0.46 − 0.24 − 0.06 = 0.16 g.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Convert to moles: nC = 0.24/12 = 0.020. nH = 0.06/1 = 0.060. nO = 0.16/16 = 0.010.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Divide by smallest (0.010): C : H : O = 2 : 6 : 1. Empirical formula: C₂H₆O.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Empirical mass = 2(12) + 6(1) + 16 = 46. Multiplier = Mᵣ / empirical mass = 46/46 = 1.`}],ans:`Molecular formula = C₂H₆O. (Could be ethanol or methoxymethane.)`},{title:`Reacting masses`,diff:`easy`,q:`What mass of MgO is produced when 4.86 g of Mg burns completely in oxygen?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: 2 Mg + O₂ → 2 MgO. Ratio Mg : MgO = 1 : 1.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles Mg = 4.86 / 24.3 = 0.200 mol. Moles MgO = 0.200 mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Mass MgO = 0.200 × 40.3 = 8.06 g.`}],ans:`Mass of MgO = 8.06 g.`},{title:`Volume of gas at RTP`,diff:`easy`,q:`What volume of H₂ at RTP is produced when 0.65 g of zinc reacts with excess dilute HCl?`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: Zn + 2 HCl → ZnCl₂ + H₂. Ratio Zn : H₂ = 1 : 1.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles Zn = 0.65 / 65 = 0.010 mol. Moles H₂ = 0.010 mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Volume H₂ at RTP = 0.010 × 24 = 0.24 dm³ = 240 cm³.`}],ans:`Volume of H₂ = 0.24 dm³ (240 cm³) at RTP.`},{title:`Limiting reagent calculation`,diff:`medium`,q:`5.85 g of NaCl is dissolved and mixed with a solution containing 17.0 g of AgNO₃. Calculate the mass of AgCl precipitate produced. (Aᵣ: Na 23, Cl 35.5, Ag 108, N 14, O 16)`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: NaCl + AgNO₃ → AgCl + NaNO₃. Ratio 1 : 1 : 1 : 1.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles NaCl = 5.85 / 58.5 = 0.100 mol. Moles AgNO₃ = 17.0 / 170 = 0.100 mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Both reactants are present in stoichiometric amounts (0.100 mol each, ratio 1:1). Neither is in excess.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`Moles AgCl produced = 0.100 mol. Mass = 0.100 × 143.5 = 14.35 g.`}],ans:`Mass of AgCl = 14.35 g (or 14.4 g to 3 sf).`},{title:`Titration calculation`,diff:`medium`,q:`A solution of NaOH was titrated against 0.100 mol/dm³ HCl. A 25.0 cm³ sample of NaOH required 22.5 cm³ of HCl to reach the endpoint. Calculate the concentration of NaOH.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: NaOH + HCl → NaCl + H₂O. Mole ratio 1 : 1.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles HCl = c × V = 0.100 × (22.5/1000) = 2.25 × 10⁻³ mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Moles NaOH = 2.25 × 10⁻³ mol (1:1 ratio).`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`[NaOH] = n / V = 2.25 × 10⁻³ / (25.0/1000) = 0.0900 mol/dm³.`}],ans:`[NaOH] = 0.0900 mol/dm³.`},{title:`Percentage yield`,diff:`easy`,q:`The decomposition of 10.0 g of CaCO₃ gives 4.20 g of CaO. Calculate the percentage yield.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles CaCO₃ = 10.0 / 100 = 0.100. Theoretical moles CaO = 0.100. Theoretical mass CaO = 0.100 × 56 = 5.60 g.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`% yield = (4.20 / 5.60) × 100 = 75.0 %.`}],ans:`75.0 %.`},{title:`Percentage purity from gas evolved`,diff:`hard`,q:`5.00 g of impure CaCO₃ reacts with excess dilute HCl. The CO₂ evolved at RTP has volume 0.960 dm³. Calculate the percentage purity of the CaCO₃.`,steps:[{label:`STEP 1`,text:`Equation: CaCO₃ + 2 HCl → CaCl₂ + H₂O + CO₂. Mole ratio CaCO₃ : CO₂ = 1 : 1.`},{label:`STEP 2`,text:`Moles CO₂ at RTP = V / 24 = 0.960 / 24 = 0.0400 mol.`},{label:`STEP 3`,text:`Moles pure CaCO₃ in sample = 0.0400 mol. Mass pure CaCO₃ = 0.0400 × 100 = 4.00 g.`},{label:`STEP 4`,text:`% purity = (4.00 / 5.00) × 100 = 80.0 %.`}],ans:`80.0 %.`}],tutorPrompts:[`Walk me through how to find an empirical formula from combustion data`,`Why is Aᵣ usually not an integer?`,`Explain how to spot the limiting reagent in a stoichiometry problem`,`How do I read M+1 and M+2 peaks in a mass spectrum?`,`Give me a tough past-paper-style titration question`]};export{e as STOICH};